WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.740
Okay so you just got back from Iceland? Where&nbsp;
you saw some geysers. So I am going to tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:14.400
about a sequence that has a lot of geysers in it,&nbsp;
it's called the Yellowstone permutation. Why it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.880
called a permutation will emerge in a minute. And&nbsp;
in this one you're actually told the first three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.900
terms and then the rules take over. Rules are&nbsp;
very simple: no term is repeated; always pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.320
the smallest legal number; the next rule says&nbsp;
that the gcd - that the greatest common divisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.800
of the term you're putting down there then and the&nbsp;
previous term has to be 1. In other words, the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.620
number has to be relatively prime to the previous&nbsp;
term. But it must have a common factor with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:53.760
term two back. Let's do it. So we're told it&nbsp;
begins 1, 2, 3 - now what can we put down for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:00.480
fourth term? The rule says it has to be relatively&nbsp;
prime to 3. Okay, so it can't be divisible by 3&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:07.440
but it must have a common factor with 2.&nbsp;
Well how about 4? 4 is the smallest thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.080
it could be because we have to always pick the&nbsp;
smallest number. So we've got 1, 2 and 3 - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.860
smallest number we could hope to use is 4, and&nbsp;
4 works. Now we're missing 5, but we can't put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.620
a 5 here because 5- even though it's relatively&nbsp;
prime to 4 doesn't have a common factor with 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:33.180
So we can't put a 5; we need a multiple of 3 in&nbsp;
fact to have a common factor with 3. So 6 would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:44.880
work, except 6 and 4 have a common factor of 2,&nbsp;
so we can't put a 6. So 7, 8, 9 - 9 works. 9 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:52.200
6 have a common factor of 3 and 9 and 4 are&nbsp;
relatively prime and no smaller number works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.880
so the fifth term is 9. Okay, now what?&nbsp;
We have to have a common factor with 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:07.320
So 6 is missing, but we can't use 6 because of the&nbsp;
9. 9 and 6 have a common factor. So the next even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:15.720
number after 6 is 8, and 8 works. And then we keep&nbsp;
going like that forever. What comes after 8? It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:23.640
got to have a common factor with 9. So a multiple&nbsp;
of 3- and it can't be 6 because of the 8 - so 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.220
6, 9. It can't be a 12 because they have-&nbsp;
12 and 8 have a factor, but 15 works. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:37.680
and what comes next? We need an even number after&nbsp;
the 8, and we don't have 6 yet but we can't use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:45.360
6 because 6 and 15 have a common factor. 2, 4, 6,&nbsp;
8 - 8 we have. We can't use 10 because of the 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:55.020
um we can't use 12 because of the 15 - 12 and&nbsp;
15 are both divisible by 3 - so it can't be 12.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:03.960
Could it be 14? Yes it could, so it's a 14 and so&nbsp;
on. And it goes like that and- (Brady: It's cool)&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:10.380
- Yeah yeah. But the interesting thing is that&nbsp;
every so often we get a big number. Let me see if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.540
we can get to a big number. Here is the next few&nbsp;
terms: finally we've got- we get the 5 that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.860
been waiting for, that was the smallest missing&nbsp;
number so I'm glad we got that. And then we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:33.660
actually fill in the 6, and then we get 25 and&nbsp;
