00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.380
numbers is the same as the sequence above it,&nbsp;
it's the sequence of integers but rearranged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:12.000
every number appears. Okay, 1: step number 1 is-&nbsp;
first of all the sequence is infinite. Because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.100
might think after a while we run out of numbers,&nbsp;
but in fact there's always a candidate, we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:23.640
pick some giant prime that hasn't appeared yet&nbsp;-
call it q - and take 15 times q. That's definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.840
going to be relatively prime to the previous&nbsp;
term, and certainly it has a common factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.240
with the two back. So there's always a candidate,&nbsp;
so the sequence is infinite. Now the next thing I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.060
need is that some numbers might be missing and&nbsp;
some numbers will be present, and right now we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:47.280
don't know. So let me define- this this is really&nbsp;
a subtle point but it's very a very key step. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.940
want to know when does a number appear? When does&nbsp;
8 appear? Well it appears as a sixth term. So let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:07:03.720
me define W for when, W of m is when m appears.&nbsp;
W of 8 is 6, W of 15 is 7 and so on. But suppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:12.600
101 is missing; then we define W of m to be -1&nbsp;
if m is missing. And I want to define L of m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:21.240
to be the max, the maximum, of W of 1 up to W of&nbsp;
m. The point is that by the time we've gone out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:28.020
W of m, every number that appears in the sequence&nbsp;
has appeared. This is the maximum you have to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:34.980
out to find m. You might- m might not be there&nbsp;
but if it is- if it is- so we take the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:42.720
of W for all the numbers up to m, that's- L stands&nbsp;
for last. L is the last chance, L of m is the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.420
chance you have to see an m. Once you've gone&nbsp;
out before beyond L of m you're in the outback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:57.420
there are no more L of m's. So for n bigger than&nbsp;
L of m, a of n is bigger than m. Somebody knows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:04.260
so that's how far we have to go out there. Now we&nbsp;
can actually do some reasoning. I claim there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:11.280
infinitely many primes that divide the terms&nbsp;
of the sequence. Proof: suppose not. Suppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:18.600
prime p on, all primes are missing. And then from&nbsp;
then on all the terms are products of primes less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:25.740
than P. So I want to look at L of p squared okay?&nbsp;
I'm going to go out a long way in the sequence;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:32.280
beyond that point we know every number is at least&nbsp;
p squared. So a of n is bigger than p squared;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:38.100
we don't know what it is but we know it's pretty&nbsp;
big. And this argument about it being pretty big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:45.660
is the key step, that's that's the key step number&nbsp;
2 that it says the sequence keeps growing. So go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:53.100
out beyond the last prime less than p squared and&nbsp;
look at what we have. So we get a term a of n,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.240
and we know it's bigger than p squared. We&nbsp;
we don't know what the number before it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.940
or the number two back but we know there was a&nbsp;
term before it that had a common factor with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:13.200
as by the definition of the sequence. And so that-&nbsp;
the linking term, the gcd, is a prime less than p&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:20.400
because from now on there are no other&nbsp;
primes. So the gcd here is say equal to q,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:26.220
say, and we know q is less than p because all&nbsp;
the terms in the sequence from then on a product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:33.060
so primes less than p. So that means that qp is&nbsp;
less than p squared - q is less than p, q times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:39.960
p is less than p squared. But then because this q&nbsp;
is less than p we could have used q times p here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.300
instead of p squared. And this is&nbsp;
a contradiction to the fact that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:52.980
always take the smallest candidate. So by&nbsp;
assuming that there was some limit to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:57.660
primes that appear we get a contradiction;
therefore there are infinitely many primes.

